Hmm, I can't point you to a good one actually. If there are some special=20
things you would like to know, perhaps I can answer.

Here are some things that I value:
Gravity is very good for off-line reading. You can tell it to download=20
all article bodies. This means you will be able to score articles based=20
on their bodies and not only headers. You will also be able to search=20
through the body texts of all your downloaded articles.
Gravity has a good scoring system, where you can add or subtract points=20
from articles based on their headers/content. The resulting score can=20
then be used with filtering and display options.
Gravity has good filtering abilities, which combines scoring and other=20
information to decide which articles/threads are included in your view.
Gravity has intrinsic ROT-13 support.
Gravity has a GUI that I find much to my liking.
Uhm, and zillions of other things... :)

If I would say something against it, it would be that it is not for=20
users that are afraid of options and customization. The powerful=20
customization features of Gravity is its main point IMHO, but obviosuly=20
they require a corresponding amount of configuration to work optimally.

Here is a, somewhat outdated, GNKSA evaluation for Gravity (v2.10,=20
1998):
http://www.newsreaders.com/gnksa/Evaluations/gravity-2.10b3.txt


